You take a daily multivitamin believing it keeps you healthy. For some people, it does. For others, evidence suggests it makes no difference
Despite multivitamin-mineral (MVM) supplements being an $11 billion global market, evidence does not support routine MVM use for well-nourished individuals eating balanced diets. Yet 40–50% of adults aged 50+ regularly take these products.
At the Institute of Nutrition and Fitness Sciences (INFS), this blog explores evidence-based answers: Are multivitamins necessary? And when does supplementation actually make sense? Understanding the difference between marketing claims and science proves essential for informed health decisions.
The Truth About Multivitamin Use
Multivitamin use is widespread. But paradoxically, the people who take them most, dont actually need them.
Research shows MVM users typically have better diet quality and fewer micronutrient deficiencies compared to non-users. This raises an important question: Do healthy people need multivitamins? The answer is not necessarily.
Most people taking MVMs are already getting adequate nutrition from food. They are most often, essentially taking supplements they do not need.
The rationale for widespread routine MVM use remains unclear when no documented deficiencies exist. The pattern is driven by marketing rather than medical necessity
What Research Actually Shows About Multivitamins
Large-scale clinical trials provide clear evidence.
Mortality And Disease Prevention
A pooled analysis from 450,000 participants found no association between MVM use and:
Reduced all-cause mortality
Cardiovascular disease mortality
Cancer mortality
The Physicians Health Study II tracked 14,641 men over 10 years. Daily MVM supplementation produced no significant reduction in major cardiovascular events (heart attack, stroke, CVD death).
The Womens Health Initiative found no protective effect against cardiovascular disease or cancer in postmenopausal women.
Early Mortality Risk: An Important Finding
Some studies reported a 4% increase in mortality hazard ratio during initial follow-up years among MVM users compared to non-users. This association attenuated over time. This suggests MVMs are not entirely risk-free, especially in the short term.
Cancer Prevention
The Physicians Health Study II reported an 8% reduction in total cancer incidence. This reduction was primarily driven by decreased prostate cancer risk. Subsequent analyses have not confirmed consistent protective effects across cancer types.
Cognitive Function
A 12-year study of 5,947 men aged 65+ found no benefit of MVM supplementation on:
Cognitive decline
Memory impairment
Verbal memory
Bottom Line: Well-nourished individuals do not derive protective benefits from routine MVM supplementation.
When Multivitamins Provide Real Benefits
While routine supplementation does not benefit well-nourished populations, multivitamins are essential tools for specific groups.Targeted supplementation dramatically improves health outcomes for people with:
Documented deficiencies (confirmed by blood tests)
Malabsorption disorders
Restricted diets
Real-world benefits after testing and medical consultation:
Patients with celiac disease who supplement vitamin D show improved bone density. Vegans supplementing B12 maintain normal neurological function and energy production. People with iron deficiency show restored energy and improved cognitive function after supplementation.
These are measurable improvements in specific clinical situations, but only when supplementation is guided by testing and doctor consultation.
The key distinction:
Targeted supplementation works for those who need it
Broad-spectrum multivitamins in healthy people do not work
Single-nutrient supplementation for confirmed deficiencies is evidence-based medicine
All supplementation requires blood testing and healthcare provider guidance
The problem often is routine supplementation in people who do not need it, or supplementation without medical oversight.
